{"authored" [{"question" "q-181650" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following sets are infinite?\n\n- $A={{f}^{(n)}(x)=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)\\,:\\,n\\in {N}_{0}}$\n- $B={{g}^{(n)}(x)=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\,:\\,n\\in {N}_{0}}$\n- $C={{h}^{(n)}(x)=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x)\\,:\\,n\\in {N}_{0}}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14035241/state.json" "file_sha256" "36a375ee6ee6aed3cd4a02d9a41385a1beb1356ae26e4b0633f09eef4476b2e5" "basis" 1237 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14035241/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-181650" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The cardinality of a finite set is the number of elements contained within it. A set is infinite if it has infinitely many elements.\n\n- The set $A$ is infinite. Listing the first few elements of our set, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{(0)}(x) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x \\\\ {f}^{(1)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x \\\\ {f}^{(2)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)=-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x \\\\ {f}^{(3)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)=8\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x \\\\ ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we can write our set as follows:\n\n$$\nA={\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x,-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x,-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x,8\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x,…}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $A$ is infinite.\n\n- The set $B$ is finite. Listing the first few elements of our set, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{(0)}(x) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ {g}^{(1)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {g}^{(2)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ {g}^{(3)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {g}^{(4)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $B$ contains four elements:\n\n$$\nB={-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $|B|=4,$ which is finite.\n\n- The set $C$ is infinite. Listing the first few elements of our set, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{(0)}(x) & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡x \\\\ {h}^{(1)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x)=\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x \\\\ {h}^{(2)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)=2{sec}^{3}⁡x-\\operatorname{sec}⁡x \\\\ ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we can write our set as follows:\n\n$$\nC={\\operatorname{sec}⁡x,\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x,2{sec}^{3}⁡x-\\operatorname{sec}⁡x,…}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $C$ is infinite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$A$ and $C$ only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14035241/state.json" "file_sha256" "36a375ee6ee6aed3cd4a02d9a41385a1beb1356ae26e4b0633f09eef4476b2e5" "basis" 1237 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14035241/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-181650" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14035241/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d8e941685c2fbed9e57620ad4338bcc7ffd9b578b42c995784607c9dcd16532a" "basis" 1237 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/