{"question" "q-181599" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following sets are infinite?\n\n- $A={{f}^{(n)}(x)=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}({x}^{2})\\,:\\,n\\in {N}_{0}}$\n- $B={{g}^{(n)}(x)=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)\\,:\\,n\\in {N}_{0}}$\n- $C={{h}^{(n)}(x)=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}(\\sqrt{x})\\,:\\,n\\in {N}_{0}}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040284/state.json" "file_sha256" "5fbd9682298969335dcbc8e0642bf48e91a65c8ef4512ffd363f581f65504528" "basis" 1424 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040284/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-181599" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The cardinality of a finite set is the number of elements contained within it. A set is infinite if it has infinitely many elements. \n\n- The set $A$ is finite. Listing a few elements of our set, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{(0)}(x) & ={x}^{2} \\\\ {f}^{(1)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2})=2x \\\\ {f}^{(2)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2x)=2 \\\\ {f}^{(3)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2)=0 \\\\ {f}^{(4)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(0)=0 \\\\  & ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, for $n>3,$ we have ${f}^{(n)}(x)=0.$ Listing a few elements of our set, we have the following:\n\n$$\nA={{x}^{2},2x,2,0}.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $|A|=4,$ which is finite.\n\n- The set $B$ is finite. Listing the first few elements of our set, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{(0)}(x) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x \\\\ {g}^{(1)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)=\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ {g}^{(2)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{x})=-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ {g}^{(3)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})=\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}} \\\\ ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we can write our set as follows:\n\n$$\nB={\\operatorname{ln}⁡x,\\frac{1}{x},-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}},-\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}},…}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $B$ is infinite.\n\n- The set $C$ is infinite. Similar to the previous case, listing the first few elements of our set, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{(0)}(x) & =\\sqrt{x} \\\\ {h}^{(1)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\sqrt{x})=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}} \\\\ {h}^{(2)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}})=-\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}} \\\\ {h}^{(3)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}})=\\frac{3}{8\\sqrt{{x}^{5}}} \\\\ ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we can write our set as follows:\n\n$$\nC={\\sqrt{x},\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}},-\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}},\\frac{3}{8\\sqrt{{x}^{5}}},…}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $C$ is infinite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$B$ and $C$ only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040284/state.json" "file_sha256" "5fbd9682298969335dcbc8e0642bf48e91a65c8ef4512ffd363f581f65504528" "basis" 1424 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040284/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-181599" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040284/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e146cf3342d1a448b3bcc321380e2d6f73a85d06a1d9ec18bdd36ec307186b3d" "basis" 1424 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040284/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-181599" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_A