r/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040284/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-181643" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The cardinality of a finite set is the number of elements contained within it. A set is infinite if it has infinitely many elements.\n\n- The set $A$ is finite. Listing the first few elements of our set, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{(0)}(x) & ={e}^{x-1} \\\\ {f}^{(1)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{x-1})={e}^{x-1} \\\\ {f}^{(2)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{x-1})={e}^{x-1} \\\\ {f}^{(3)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{x-1})={e}^{x-1} \\\\ ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $A$ contains only one element:\n\n$$\nA={{e}^{x-1}}.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $|A|=1,$ which is finite.\n\n- The set $B$ is infinite. Listing the first few elements of our set, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{(0)}(x) & ={e}^{-2x} \\\\ {g}^{(1)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{-2x})=-2{e}^{-2x} \\\\ {g}^{(2)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-2{e}^{-2x})=4{e}^{-2x} \\\\ {g}^{(3)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4{e}^{-2x})=-8{e}^{-2x} \\\\ ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we can write our set as follows:\n\n$$\nB={{e}^{-2x},-2{e}^{-2x},4{e}^{-2x},-8{e}^{-2x},…}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $B$ is infinite.\n\n- The set $C$ is infinite. Listing the first few elements of our set, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{(0)}(x) & ={e}^{x/2} \\\\ {h}^{(1)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{x/2})=\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x/2} \\\\ {h}^{(2)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x/2})=\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{x/2} \\\\ {h}^{(3)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{x/2})=\\frac{1}{8}{e}^{x/2} \\\\ ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we can write our set as follows:\n\n$$\nC={{e}^{x/2},\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x/2},\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{x/2},\\frac{1}{8}{e}^{x/2},…}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $C$ is infinite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$B$ and $C$ only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040284/state.json" "file_sha256" "5fbd9682298969335dcbc8e0642bf48e91a65c8ef4512ffd363f581f65504528" "basis" 1424 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040284/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-181643" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040284/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e146cf3342d1a448b3bcc321380e2d6f73a85d06a1d9ec18bdd36ec307186b3d" "basis" 1424 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040284/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-181643" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040284/state.json" "file_sha256" "5fbd9682298969335dcbc8e0642bf48e91a65c8ef4512ffd363f581f65504528" "basis" 1424 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040284/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-181643" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "$A$ and $B$ only"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040284/state.json" "file_sha256" "5fbd9682298969335dcbc8e0642bf48e91a65c8ef4512ffd363f581f65504528" "basis" 1424 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040284/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-181643" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "$A$ and $C$ only"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040284/state.json" "file_sha256" "5fbd9682298969335dcbc8e0642bf48e91a65c8ef4512ffd363f581f65504528" "basis" 1424 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040284/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-181643" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "$B$ and $C$ only"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040284/state.json" "file_sha256" "5fbd9682298969335dcbc8e0642bf48e91a65c8ef4512ffd363f581f65504528" "basis" 1424 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040284/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-181643" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "$B$ only"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040284/state.json" "file_sha256" "5fbd9682298969335dcbc8e0642bf48e91a65c8ef4512ffd363f581f65504528" "basis" 1424 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040284/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-181643" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "$C$ only"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040284/state.json" "file_sha256" "5fbd9682298969335dcbc8e0642bf48e91a65c8ef4512ffd363f581f65504528" "basis" 1424 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040284/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-181643" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$B$ and $C$ only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040284/state.json" "file_sha256" "5fbd9682298969335dcbc8e0642bf48e91a65c8ef4512ffd363f581f65504528" "basis" 1424 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040284/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-13171" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following sets are infinite?\n\n- $A = \\{n \\in \\mathbb{Z} \\: : \\: n^2-9 \\geq 0 \\}$\n\n- $B = \\{m \\in \\mathbb{Z} \\: : \\: m^2-9 \\leq 0 \\}$\n\n- $C = \\{q \\in \\mathbb{Q} \\: : \\: q^2-9 \\leq 0 \\}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040284/example-13171.json" "file_sha256" "4364622df985db78c7b21ffe860d847440809770338f45f6ec9318d820a6458e" "basis" 1424 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040284/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-13171" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The cardinality of a finite set is the number of elements contained within it. A set is infinite if it has infinitely many elements.\n\nLet's start by analyzing the parabola $y=x^2-9$ for $x\\in\\mathbb R.$\n\nFirst, we find the roots of the parabola:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-9 & =0 \\\\ (x-3)(x+3) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the roots are $x=-3$ and $x=3,$ and we can graph the parabola as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040284/assets/3c2fc2f1eeb1d4e92f7933a408dafe7c3a216aa3d899f4025c71407140d47de1.png)\n\nTherefore:\n\n- The solution to the inequality $x^2-9 \\geq 0$ for $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$ is \n\n$$\nx \\leq -3 \\quad\\text{or}\\quad x \\geq 3\n$$\n\nand can be expressed as $x \\in I_1 = \\left(-\\infty, -3\\right] \\cup \\left[3,\\infty\\right).$\n\n- Similarly, the solution to the inequality $x^2-9 \\leq 0$ for $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$ is \n\n$$\n-3 \\leq x \\leq 3\n$$\n\nand can be expressed as $x \\in I_2 = [-3,3].$\n\nWith these in mind, let's examine our sets:\n\n- The set $A$ is infinite. If $n \\in \\mathbb{Z},$ the solutions to the inequality $n^2-9 \\geq 0$ are all the integers that lie in the interval $I_1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nA = \\{ \\ldots, -4, -3 \\} \\cup \\{ 3, 4, \\ldots \\}.\n$$\n\n- The set $B$ is finite. If $m \\in \\mathbb{Z},$ the solutions to the inequality $m^2-9 \\leq 0$ are all the integers that lie in the interval $I_2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nB = \\{0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,\\pm 3\\}.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $|B| = 7,$ which is finite.\n\n- The set $C$ is infinite. If $q \\in \\mathbb{Q},$ the solutions to $q^2-9 \\leq 0$ are all the rational numbers that lie in the interval $I_2.$ Since any real interval of nonzero length contains infinitely many rational numbers, it follows that $C$ is infinite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$A$ and $C$ only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040284/example-13171.json" "file_sha256" "4364622df985db78c7b21ffe860d847440809770338f45f6ec9318d820a6458e" "basis" 1424 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040284/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-13174" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following sets are infinite?\n\n- $A = \\{n \\in \\mathbb{Z} \\: : \\: \\sin (n\\pi) = 1 \\}$\n\n- $B = \\{q \\in \\mathbb{Q} \\: : \\: \\sin (q\\pi) = 1 \\}$\n\n- $C = \\{x \\in \\mathbb{R} \\: : \\: \\sin (x\\pi) = 1 \\}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040284/example-13174.json" "file_sha256" "d3d04daa0535db6995dbcb148973ebf092a19603c7aba1dc36b534e03e847739" "basis" 1424 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040284/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-13174" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The cardinality of a finite set is the number of elements contained within it. A set is infinite if it has infinitely many elements.\n\nWe start by finding the solutions to the equation $\\sin (x\\pi) = 1$ for $x\\in\\mathbb R{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\pi  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}+2m\\pi  \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{2}+2m,\\,m\\in Z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our sets:\n\n- The set $A$ is finite. There are no values of $m\\in\\mathbb Z$ such that $\\dfrac{1}{2} + 2m \\in\\mathbb Z.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nA = \\{\\}.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $|A| = 0,$ which is finite.\n\n- The set $B$ is infinite. Listing the elements of $B$ for different values of $m,$ we have\n\n$$\nB = \\left\\{\\ldots,-\\dfrac{3}{2},\\dfrac12, \\dfrac{5}{2}, \\dfrac{9}{2},\\ldots\\right\\}.\n$$\n\n- The set $C$ is infinite. Since $B$ is infinite, and $B \\subseteq C,$ it follows that $C$ is infinite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$B$ and $C$ only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040284/example-13174.json" "file_sha256" "d3d04daa0535db6995dbcb148973ebf092a19603c7aba1dc36b534e03e847739" "basis" 1424 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040284/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-13175" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following sets are infinite?\n\n- $A = \\left\\{f^{(n)}(x) = \\dfrac{\\textrm d^n}{\\textrm d x^n}(e^{5x})  \\: : \\:  n\\in \\mathbb{N}_0 \\right\\}$\n\n- $B = \\left\\{g^{(n)}(x) = \\dfrac{\\textrm d^n}{\\textrm d x^n}(6e^{-x})  \\: : \\:  n\\in \\mathbb{N}_0 \\right\\}$\n\n- $C =  \\left\\{h^{(n)}(x) = \\dfrac{\\textrm d^n}{\\textrm d x^n}(2e^{x+3})  \\: : \\:  n\\in \\mathbb{N}_0 \\right\\}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040284/example-13175.json" "file_sha256" "6c8ed700bc87a8a3b2fe3832976ba1669a43ee423bb9676a60505b95da910b57" "basis" 1424 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040284/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-13175" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The cardinality of a finite set is the number of elements contained within it. A set is infinite if it has infinitely many elements.\n\n- The set $A$ is infinite. Listing the first few elements of our set, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{(0)}(x) & ={e}^{5x} \\\\ {f}^{(1)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{5x})=5{e}^{5x} \\\\ {f}^{(2)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(5{e}^{5x})=25{e}^{5x} \\\\ {f}^{(3)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(25{e}^{5x})=125{e}^{5x} \\\\ ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we can write our set as follows:\n\n$$\nA = \\{e^{5x}, 5e^{5x}, 25e^{5x}, 125e^{5x}, \\ldots\\}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $A$ is infinite.\n\n- The set $B$ is finite. Listing the first few elements of our set, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{(0)}(x) & =6{e}^{-x} \\\\ {g}^{(1)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(6{e}^{-x})=-6{e}^{-x} \\\\ {g}^{(2)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-6{e}^{-x})=6{e}^{-x} \\\\ {g}^{(3)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(6{e}^{-x})=-6{e}^{-x} \\\\ ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $B$ contains only two elements:\n\n$$\nB = \\{6e^{-x}, -6e^{-x}\\}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $|B| = 2,$ which is finite.\n\n- The set $C$ is also finite. Listing the first few elements of our set, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{(0)}(x) & =2{e}^{x+3} \\\\ {h}^{(1)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{e}^{x+3})=2{e}^{x+3} \\\\ {h}^{(2)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{e}^{x+3})=2{e}^{x+3} \\\\ {h}^{(3)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{e}^{x+3})=2{e}^{x+3} \\\\ ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $C$ contains only one element:\n\n$$\nC = \\{ 2e^{x+3} \\}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $|C| = 1,$ which is finite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$A$ only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040284/example-13175.json" "file_sha256" "6c8ed700bc87a8a3b2fe3832976ba1669a43ee423bb9676a60505b95da910b57" "basis" 1424 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040284/edb-import/verification.json"}] "sources" [{"question" "q-181733" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following sets are infinite?\n\n- $A={n\\in Z\\,:\\,|4n|\\ge 8}$\n- $B={q\\in Q\\,:\\,|4q|\\ge 8}$\n- $C={x\\in R\\,:\\,|4x|\\ge 8}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072312/state.json" "file_sha256" "6140f55f6390ef42e187b2506123ae6d00a247c17ed95d16548875b1f5c2cc19"} {"question" "q-181733" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The cardinality of a finite set is the number of elements contained within it. A set is infinite if it has infinitely many elements. \n\nWe start by solving the following inequality for $x\\in R:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|4x|\\ge 8 \\\\ 4|x|\\ge 8 \\\\ |x|\\ge 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\nx\\le -2\\,\\text{or}\\,x\\ge 2\n$$\n\nand can be expressed as $x\\in I=(-\\infty ,-2]\\cup [2,\\infty ).$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our sets:\n\n- The set $A$ is infinite. If $n\\in Z,$ the solutions to the inequality $|4n|\\ge 8$ are all the integers that lie in the interval $I.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\nA={…,-3,-2}\\cup {2,3,…}.\n$$\n\n- The set $B$ is infinite. If $q\\in Q,$ the solutions to $|4q|\\ge 8$ are all the rational numbers that lie in the interval $I.$ Since any real interval of nonzero length contains infinitely many rational numbers, it follows that $B$ is infinite.\n\n- The set $C$ is infinite. Since $B$ is infinite, and $B⊂C,$ it follows that $C$ is infinite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$A,B,$ and $C.$\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072312/state.json" "file_sha256" "6140f55f6390ef42e187b2506123ae6d00a247c17ed95d16548875b1f5c2cc19"} {"question" "q-181733" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072312/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "fe506a8344931eb94d2a69cfa1037c318d4c5c21aa2e1c09a84b9ff51e3215a5"} {"question" "q-181733" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072312/state.json" "file_sha256" "6140f55f6390ef42e187b2506123ae6d00a247c17ed95d16548875b1f5c2cc19"} {"question" "q-181733" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$A$ only"] ["text" "$A,B,$ and $C$"] ["text" "$B$ and $C$ only"] ["text" "$C$ only"] ["text" "None of the sets are infinite"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072312/state.json" "file_sha256" "6140f55f6390ef42e187b2506123ae6d00a247c17ed95d16548875b1f5c2cc19"} {"question" "q-181733" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$A,B,$ and $C$"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072312/state.json" "file_sha256" "6140f55f6390ef42e187b2506123ae6d00a247c17ed95d16548875b1f5c2cc19"} {"question" "q-181721" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following sets are infinite?\n\n- $A={n\\in Z\\,:\\,|5n|\\le 4}$\n- $B={q\\in Q\\,:\\,|5q|\\le 4}$\n- $C={x\\in R\\,:\\,|5x|\\le 4}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072312/state.json" "file_sha256" "6140f55f6390ef42e187b2506123ae6d00a247c17ed95d16548875b1f5c2cc19"} {"question" "q-181721" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The cardinality of a finite set is the number of elements contained within it. A set is infinite if it has infinitely many elements.\n\nWe start by solving the following inequality for $x\\in R:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|5x|\\le 4 \\\\ 5|x|\\le 4 \\\\ |x|\\le \\frac{4}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n-\\frac{4}{5}\\le x\\le \\frac{4}{5}\n$$\n\nand can be expressed as $x\\in I=[-\\frac{4}{5},\\frac{4}{5}].$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our sets:\n\n- The set $A$ is finite. If $n\\in Z,$ the solutions to the inequality $|5n|\\le 4$ are all the integers that lie in the interval $I.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\nA={0}.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $|A|=1,$ which is finite.\n\n- The set $B$ is infinite. If $q\\in Q,$ the solutions to $|5q|\\le 4$ are all the rational numbers that lie in the interval $I.$ Since any real interval of nonzero length contains infinitely many rational numbers, it follows that $B$ is infinite.\n\n- The set $C$ is infinite. Since $B$ is infinite, and $B⊂C,$ it follows that $C$ is infinite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$B$ and $C$ only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072312/state.json" "file_sha256" "6140f55f6390ef42e187b2506123ae6d00a247c17ed95d16548875b1f5c2cc19"} {"question" "q-181721" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072312/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "fe506a8344931eb94d2a69cfa1037c318d4c5c21aa2e1c09a84b9ff51e3215a5"} {"question" "q-181721" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072312/state.json" "file_sha256" "6140f55f6390ef42e187b2506123ae6d00a247c17ed95d16548875b1f5c2cc19"} {"question" "q-181721" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$A$ and $B$ only"] ["text" "$A$ and $C$ only"] ["text" "$A,B,$ and $C$ only"] ["text" "$B$ and $C$ only"] ["text" "$C$ only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072312/state.json" "file_sha256" "6140f55f6390ef42e187b2506123ae6d00a247c17ed95d16548875b1f5c2cc19"} {"question" "q-181721" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$B$ and $C$ only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072312/state.json" "file_sha256" "6140f55f6390ef42e187b2506123ae6d00a247c17ed95d16548875b1f5c2cc19"} {"question" "q-181740" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following sets are infinite?\n\n- $A={n\\in Z\\,:\\,-{n}^{2}+1>0}$\n- $B={m\\in Z\\,:\\,-{m}^{2}+1<0}$\n- $C={q\\in Q\\,:\\,-{q}^{2}+1<0}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072312/state.json" "file_sha256" "6140f55f6390ef42e187b2506123ae6d00a247c17ed95d16548875b1f5c2cc19"} {"question" "q-181740" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The cardinality of a finite set is the number of elements contained within it. A set is infinite if it has infinitely many elements. \n\nLet's start by analyzing the parabola $y=-{x}^{2}+1$ for $x\\in R.$\n\nFirst, we find the roots of the parabola:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}^{2}+1 & =0 \\\\ -({x}^{2}-1) & =0 \\\\ -(x-1)(x+1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the roots are $x=-1$ and $x=1,$ and we can graph the parabola as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072312/assets/0699524ff20f871c9eeca93faa9934832b8e5816fd23b46862e8b01d48e7d86b.png)\n\nTherefore:\n\n- The solution to the inequality $-{x}^{2}+1>0$ for $x\\in R$ is \n\n$$\n-1<x<1\n$$\n\nand can be expressed as $x\\in {I}_{1}=(-1,1).$\n\n- Similarly, the solution to the inequality $-{x}^{2}+1<0$ for $x\\in R$ is \n\n$$\nx<-1\\,\\text{or}\\,x>1\n$$\n\nand can be expressed as $x\\in {I}_{2}=(-\\infty ,-1)\\cup (1,\\infty ).$\n\nWith these in mind, let's examine our sets:\n\n- The set $A$ is finite. If $n\\in Z,$ the solutions to the inequality $-{n}^{2}+1>0$ are all the integers that lie in the interval ${I}_{1}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nA={0}.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $|A|=1,$ which is finite.\n\n- The set $B$ is infinite. If $m\\in Z,$ the solutions to the inequality $-{m}^{2}+1<0$ are all the integers that lie in the interval ${I}_{2}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nB={…,-3,-2}\\cup {2,3,…}.\n$$\n\n- The set $C$ is infinite. If $q\\in Q,$ the solutions to $-{q}^{2}+1<0$ are all the rational numbers that lie in the interval ${I}_{2}.$ Since any real interval of nonzero length contains infinitely many rational numbers, it follows that $C$ is infinite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$B$ and $C$ only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072312/state.json" "file_sha256" "6140f55f6390ef42e187b2506123ae6d00a247c17ed95d16548875b1f5c2cc19"} {"question" "q-181740" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072312/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "fe506a8344931eb94d2a69cfa1037c318d4c5c21aa2e1c09a84b9ff51e3215a5"} {"question" "q-181740" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072312/state.json" "file_sha256" "6140f55f6390ef42e187b2506123ae6d00a247c17ed95d16548875b1f5c2cc19"} {"question" "q-181740" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$A$ and $B$ only"] ["text" "$A$ and $C$ only"] ["text" "$A,B,$ and $C$"] ["text" "$B$ and $C$ only"] ["text" "$C$ only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072312/state.json" "file_sha256" "6140f55f6390ef42e187b2506123ae6d00a247c17ed95d16548875b1f5c2cc19"} {"question" "q-181740" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$B$ and $C$ only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072312/state.json" "file_sha256" "6140f55f6390ef42e187b2506123ae6d00a247c17ed95d16548875b1f5c2cc19"} {"question" "q-181536" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following sets are infinite?\n\n- $A={n\\in Z\\,:\\,{n}^{2}-5n+6>0}$\n- $B={m\\in Z\\,:\\,{m}^{2}-5m+6<0}$\n- $C={q\\in Q\\,:\\,{q}^{2}-5q+6<0}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072312/state.json" "file_sha256" "6140f55f6390ef42e187b2506123ae6d00a247c17ed95d16548875b1f5c2cc19"} {"question" "q-181536" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The cardinality of a finite set is the number of elements contained within it. A set is infinite if it has infinitely many elements. \n\nLet's start by analyzing the parabola $y={x}^{2}-5x+6$ for $x\\in R.$\n\nFirst, we find the roots of the parabola:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-5x+6 & =0 \\\\ (x-2)(x-3) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the roots are $x=2$ and $x=3,$ and we can graph the parabola as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072312/assets/0a42815f626652c332862a38a3812463a9407605329bd81df25f11274b26ce6f.png)\n\nTherefore:\n\n- The solution to the inequality ${x}^{2}-5x+6>0$ for $x\\in R$ is \n\n$$\nx<2\\,\\text{or}\\,x>3\n$$\n\nand can be expressed as $x\\in {I}_{1}=(-\\infty ,2)\\cup (3,\\infty ).$\n\n- Similarly, the solution to the inequality ${x}^{2}-5x+6<0$ for $x\\in R$ is \n\n$$\n2<x<3\n$$\n\nand can be expressed as $x\\in {I}_{2}=(2,3).$\n\nWith these in mind, let's examine our sets:\n\n- The set $A$ is infinite. If $n\\in Z,$ the solutions to the inequality ${n}^{2}-5n+6>0$ are all the integers that lie in the interval ${I}_{1}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nA={…,0,1}\\cup {4,5,…}.\n$$\n\n- The set $B$ is finite. If $m\\in Z,$ the solutions to the inequality ${m}^{2}-5m+6<0$ are all the integers that lie in the interval ${I}_{2}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nB={}.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $|B|=0,$ which is finite.\n\n- The set $C$ is infinite. If $q\\in Q,$ the solutions to ${q}^{2}-5q+6<0$ are all the rational numbers that lie in the interval ${I}_{2}.$ Since any real interval of nonzero length contains infinitely many rational numbers, it follows that $C$ is infinite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$A$ and $C$ only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072312/state.json" "file_sha256" "6140f55f6390ef42e187b2506123ae6d00a247c17ed95d16548875b1f5c2cc19"} {"question" "q-181536" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072312/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "fe506a8344931eb94d2a69cfa1037c318d4c5c21aa2e1c09a84b9ff51e3215a5"} {"question" "q-181536" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072312/state.json" "file_sha256" "6140f55f6390ef42e187b2506123ae6d00a247c17ed95d16548875b1f5c2cc19"} {"question" "q-181536" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$A$ and $B$ only"] ["text" "$A$ and $C$ only"] ["text" "$A$ only"] ["text" "$B$ and $C$ only"] ["text" "$C$ only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072312/state.json" "file_sha256" "6140f55f6390ef42e187b2506123ae6d00a247c17ed95d16548875b1f5c2cc19"} {"question" "q-181536" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$A$ and $C$ only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072312/state.json" "file_sha256" "6140f55f6390ef42e187b2506123ae6d00a247c17ed95d16548875b1f5c2cc19"} {"question" "q-181768" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following sets are infinite?\n\n- $A={n\\in Z\\,:\\,{n}^{2}-2n\\le 0}$\n- $B={m\\in Z\\,:\\,{m}^{2}-2m\\ge 0}$\n- $C={q\\in Q\\,:\\,{q}^{2}-2q\\le 0}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072312/state.json" "file_sha256" "6140f55f6390ef42e187b2506123ae6d00a247c17ed95d16548875b1f5c2cc19"} {"question" "q-181768" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The cardinality of a finite set is the number of elements contained within it. A set is infinite if it has infinitely many elements. \n\nLet's start by analyzing the parabola $y={x}^{2}-2x$ for $x\\in R.$\n\nFirst, we find the roots of the parabola:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-2x & =0 \\\\ x(x-2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the roots are $x=0$ and $x=2,$ and we can graph the parabola as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072312/assets/496c6d53993ca6cae499f617bca42dbc42e4c57bffa278da6312a5b1723bdcbc.png)\n\nTherefore:\n\n- The solution to the inequality ${x}^{2}-2x\\le 0$ for $x\\in R$ is \n\n$$\n0\\le x\\le 2\n$$\n\nand can be expressed as $x\\in {I}_{1}=[0,2].$\n\n- Similarly, the solution to the inequality ${x}^{2}-2x\\ge 0$ for $x\\in R$ is \n\n$$\nx\\le 0\\,\\text{or}\\,x\\ge 2\n$$\n\nand can be expressed as $x\\in {I}_{2}=(-\\infty ,0]\\cup [2,\\infty ).$\n\nWith these in mind, let's examine our sets:\n\n- The set $A$ is finite. If $n\\in Z,$ the solutions to the inequality ${n}^{2}-2n\\le 0$ are all the integers that lie in the interval ${I}_{1}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nA={0,1,2}.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $|A|=3,$ which is finite.\n\n- The set $B$ is infinite. If $m\\in Z,$ the solutions to the inequality ${m}^{2}-2m\\ge 0$ are all the integers that lie in the interval ${I}_{2}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nB={…,-1,0}\\cup {2,3,…}.\n$$\n\n- The set $C$ is infinite. If $q\\in Q,$ the solutions to ${q}^{2}-2q\\le 0$ are all the rational numbers that lie in the interval ${I}_{1}.$ Since any real interval of nonzero length contains infinitely many rational numbers, it follows that $C$ is infinite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$B$ and $C$ only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072312/state.json" "file_sha256" "6140f55f6390ef42e187b2506123ae6d00a247c17ed95d16548875b1f5c2cc19"} {"question" "q-181768" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072312/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "fe506a8344931eb94d2a69cfa1037c318d4c5c21aa2e1c09a84b9ff51e3215a5"} {"question" "q-181768" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072312/state.json" "file_sha256" "6140f55f6390ef42e187b2506123ae6d00a247c17ed95d16548875b1f5c2cc19"} {"question" "q-181768" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$A$ and $B$ only"] ["text" "$A$ and $C$ only"] ["text" "$B$ and $C$ only"] ["text" "$B$ only"] ["text" "$C$ only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072312/state.json" "file_sha256" "6140f55f6390ef42e187b2506123ae6d00a247c17ed95d16548875b1f5c2cc19"} {"question" "q-181768" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$B$ and $C$ only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072312/state.json" "file_sha256" "6140f55f6390ef42e187b2506123ae6d00a247c17ed95d16548875b1f5c2cc19"} {"question" "q-181484" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following sets are infinite?\n\n- $A={n\\in Z\\,:\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡n=1}$\n- $B={q\\in Q\\,:\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡q=1}$\n- $C={x\\in R\\,:\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=1}$\n\nHint: The arguments of the trigonometric functions are in radians." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072312/state.json" "file_sha256" "6140f55f6390ef42e187b2506123ae6d00a247c17ed95d16548875b1f5c2cc19"} {"question" "q-181484" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The cardinality of a finite set is the number of elements contained within it. A set is infinite if it has infinitely many elements.\n\nWe start by writing down the solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=1$ for $x\\in R:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+2m\\pi ,\\,m\\in Z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our sets:\n\n- The set $A$ is finite. There are no values of $m\\in Z$ such that $\\frac{\\pi }{2}+2m\\pi \\in Z.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nA={}.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $|A|=0,$ which is finite.\n\n- The set $B$ is finite. Similar to the above, there are no values of $m\\in Z$ such 