\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{x})={e}^{x} \\\\ {f}^{(3)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{x})={e}^{x} \\\\ ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $A$ contains only one element:\n\n$$\nA={{e}^{x}}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $|A|=1,$ which is finite.\n\n- The set $B$ is finite. Similar to the previous case, $B$ contains only one element:\n\n$$\nA={2{e}^{x}}.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $|B|=1,$ which is finite.\n\n- The set $C$ is infinite. Listing the first few elements of our set, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{(0)}(x) & ={e}^{2x} \\\\ {h}^{(1)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{2x})=2{e}^{2x} \\\\ {h}^{(2)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{e}^{2x})=4{e}^{2x} \\\\ {h}^{(3)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4{e}^{2x})=8{e}^{2x} \\\\ ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we can write our set as follows:\n\n$$\nC={{e}^{2x},2{e}^{2x},4{e}^{2x},8{e}^{2x},…}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $C$ is infinite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$C$ only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072312/state.json" "file_sha256" "6140f55f6390ef42e187b2506123ae6d00a247c17ed95d16548875b1f5c2cc19"} {"question" "q-181485" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072312/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "fe506a8344931eb94d2a69cfa1037c318d4c5c21aa2e1c09a84b9ff51e3215a5"} {"question" "q-181485" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072312/state.json" "file_sha256" "6140f55f6390ef42e187b2506123ae6d00a247c17ed95d16548875b1f5c2cc19"} {"question" "q-181485" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$A$ only"] ["text" "$A,B$ and $C$"] ["text" "$B$ and $C$ only"] ["text" "$C$ only"] ["text" "None of the sets are infinite."]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072312/state.json" "file_sha256" "6140f55f6390ef42e187b2506123ae6d00a247c17ed95d16548875b1f5c2cc19"} {"question" "q-181485" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$C$ only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072312/state.json" "file_sha256" "6140f55f6390ef42e187b2506123ae6d00a247c17ed95d16548875b1f5c2cc19"} {"question" "q-181653" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following sets are infinite?\n\n- $A={{f}^{(n)}(x)=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\,:\\,n\\in {N}_{0}}$\n- $B={{g}^{(n)}(x)=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}(-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x)\\,:\\,n\\in {N}_{0}}$\n- $C={{h}^{(n)}(x)=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}(\\operatorname{cot}⁡x)\\,:\\,n\\in {N}_{0}}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072312/state.json" "file_sha256" "6140f55f6390ef42e187b2506123ae6d00a247c17ed95d16548875b1f5c2cc19"} {"question" "q-181653" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The cardinality of a finite set is the number of elements contained within it. A set is infinite if it has infinitely many elements.\n\n- The set $A$ is finite. Listing the first few elements of our set, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{(0)}(x) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{(1)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{(2)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{(3)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{(4)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $A$ contains four elements:\n\n$$\nA={-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $|A|=4,$ which is finite.\n\n- The set $B$ is infinite. Listing the first few elements of our set, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{(0)}(x) & =-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x \\\\ {g}^{(1)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x)=\\operatorname{cot}⁡x\\operatorname{csc}⁡x \\\\ {g}^{(2)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{cot}⁡x\\operatorname{csc}⁡x)=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x-2{csc}^{3}⁡x \\\\ ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we can write our set as follows:\n\n$$\nB={-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x,\\operatorname{cot}⁡x\\operatorname{csc}⁡x,\\operatorname{csc}⁡x-2{csc}^{3}⁡x,…}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $B$ is infinite.\n\n- The set $C$ is also infinite. Listing the first few elements of our set, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{(0)}(x) & =\\operatorname{cot}⁡x \\\\ {h}^{(1)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{cot}⁡x)=-{csc}^{2}⁡x \\\\ {h}^{(2)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-{csc}^{2}⁡x)=2\\operatorname{cot}⁡x{csc}^{2}⁡x \\\\ ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we can write our set as follows:\n\n$$\nC={\\operatorname{cot}⁡x,-{csc}^{2}⁡x,2\\operatorname{cot}⁡x{csc}^{2}⁡x,…}.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $C$ is infinite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$B$ and $C$ only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072312/state.json" "file_sha256" "6140f55f6390ef42e187b2506123ae6d00a247c17ed95d16548875b1f5c2cc19"} {"question" "q-181653" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072312/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "fe506a8344931eb94d2a69cfa1037c318d4c5c21aa2e1c09a84b9ff51e3215a5"} {"question" "q-181653" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072312/state.json" "file_sha256" "6140f55f6390ef42e187b2506123ae6d00a247c17ed95d16548875b1f5c2cc19"} {"question" "q-181653" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$A$ and $B$ only"] ["text" "$A$ only"] ["text" "$B$ and $C$ only"] ["text" "$B$ only"] ["text" "None of the sets are infinite."]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072312/state.json" "file_sha256" "6140f55f6390ef42e187b2506123ae6d00a247c17ed95d16548875b1f5c2cc19"} {"question" "q-181653" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$B$ and $C$ only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072312/state.json" "file_sha256" "6140f55f6390ef42e187b2506123ae6d00a247c17ed95d16548875b1f5c2cc19"} {"question" "q-181650" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following sets are infinite?\n\n- $A={{f}^{(n)}(x)=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)\\,:\\,n\\in {N}_{0}}$\n- $B={{g}^{(n)}(x)=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\,:\\,n\\in {N}_{0}}$\n- $C={{h}^{(n)}(x)=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x)\\,:\\,n\\in {N}_{0}}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072312/state.json" "file_sha256" "6140f55f6390ef42e187b2506123ae6d00a247c17ed95d16548875b1f5c2cc19"} {"question" "q-181650" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The cardinality of a finite set is the number of elements contained within it. A set is infinite if it has infinitely many elements.\n\n- The set $A$ is infinite. Listing the first few elements of our set, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{(0)}(x) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x \\\\ {f}^{(1)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x \\\\ {f}^{(2)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)=-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x \\\\ {f}^{(3)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)=8\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x \\\\ ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we can write our set as follows:\n\n$$\nA={\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x,-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x,-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x,8\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x,…}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $A$ is infinite.\n\n- The set $B$ is finite. Listing the first few elements of our set, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{(0)}(x) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ {g}^{(1)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {g}^{(2)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ {g}^{(3)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {g}^{(4)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $B$ contains four elements:\n\n$$\nB={-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $|B|=4,$ which is finite.\n\n- The set $C$ is infinite. Listing the first few elements of our set, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{(0)}(x) & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡x \\\\ {h}^{(1)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x)=\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x \\\\ {h}^{(2)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)=2{sec}^{3}⁡x-\\operatorname{sec}⁡x \\\\ ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we can write our set as follows:\n\n$$\nC={\\operatorname{sec}⁡x,\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x,2{sec}^{3}⁡x-\\operatorname{sec}⁡x,…}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $C$ is infinite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$A$ and $C$ only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072312/state.json" "file_sha256" "6140f55f6390ef42e187b2506123ae6d00a247c17ed95d16548875b1f5c2cc19"} {"question" "q-181650" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072312/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "fe506a8344931eb94d2a69cfa1037c318d4c5c21aa2e1c09a84b9ff51e3215a5"} {"question" "q-181650" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072312/state.json" "file_sha256" "6140f55f6390ef42e187b2506123ae6d00a247c17ed95d16548875b1f5c2cc19"} {"question" "q-181650" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$A$ and $C$ only"] ["text" "$A$ only"] ["text" "$B$ and $C$ only"] ["text" "$B$ only"] ["text" "$C$ only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072312/state.json" "file_sha256" "6140f55f6390ef42e187b2506123ae6d00a247c17ed95d16548875b1f5c2cc19"} {"question" "q-181650" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$A$ and $C$ only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072312/state.json" "file_sha256" "6140f55f6390ef42e187b2506123ae6d00a247c17ed95d16548875b1f5c2cc19"} {"question" "q-181643" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following sets are infinite?\n\n- $A={{f}^{(n)}(x)=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}({e}^{x-1})\\,:\\,n\\in {N}_{0}}$\n- $B={{g}^{(n)}(x)=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}({e}^{-2x})\\,:\\,n\\in {N}_{0}}$\n- $C={{h}^{(n)}(x)=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}({e}^{x/2})\\,:\\,n\\in {N}_{0}}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072312/state.json" "file_sha256" "6140f55f6390ef42e187b2506123ae6d00a247c17ed95d16548875b1f5c2cc19"} {"question" "q-181643" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The cardinality of a finite set is the number of elements contained within it. A set is infinite if it has infinitely many elements.\n\n- The set $A$ is finite. Listing the first few elements of our set, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{(0)}(x) & ={e}^{x-1} \\\\ {f}^{(1)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{x-1})={e}^{x-1} \\\\ {f}^{(2)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{x-1})={e}^{x-1} \\\\ {f}^{(3)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{x-1})={e}^{x-1} \\\\ ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $A$ contains only one element:\n\n$$\nA={{e}^{x-1}}.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $|A|=1,$ which is finite.\n\n- The set $B$ is infinite. Listing the first few elements of our set, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{(0)}(x) & ={e}^{-2x} \\\\ {g}^{(1)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{-2x})=-2{e}^{-2x} \\\\ {g}^{(2)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-2{e}^{-2x})=4{e}^{-2x} \\\\ {g}^{(3)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4{e}^{-2x})=-8{e}^{-2x} \\\\ ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we can write our set as follows:\n\n$$\nB={{e}^{-2x},-2{e}^{-2x},4{e}^{-2x},-8{e}^{-2x},…}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $B$ is infinite.\n\n- The set $C$ is infinite. Listing the first few elements of our set, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{(0)}(x) & ={e}^{x/2} \\\\ {h}^{(1)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{x/2})=\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x/2} \\\\ {h}^{(2)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x/2})=\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{x/2} \\\\ {h}^{(3)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{x/2})=\\frac{1}{8}{e}^{x/2} \\\\ ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we can write our set as follows:\n\n$$\nC={{e}^{x/2},\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x/2},\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{x/2},\\frac{1}{8}{e}^{x/2},…}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $C$ is infinite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$B$ and $C$ only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072312/state.json" "file_sha256" "6140f55f6390ef42e187b2506123ae6d00a247c17ed95d16548875b1f5c2cc19"} {"question" "q-181643" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072312/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "fe506a8344931eb94d2a69cfa1037c318d4c5c21aa2e1c09a84b9ff51e3215a5"} {"question" "q-181643" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072312/state.json" "file_sha256" "6140f55f6390ef42e187b2506123ae6d00a247c17ed95d16548875b1f5c2cc19"} {"question" "q-181643" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$A$ and $B$ only"] ["text" "$A$ and $C$ only"] ["text" "$B$ and $C$ only"] ["text" "$B$ only"] ["text" "$C$ only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072312/state.json" "file_sha256" "6140f55f6390ef42e187b2506123ae6d00a247c17ed95d16548875b1f5c2cc19"} {"question" "q-181643" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$B$ and $C$ only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072312/state.json" "file_sha256" "6140f55f6390ef42e187b2506123ae6d00a247c17ed95d16548875b1f5c2cc19"} {"question" "e-13171" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following sets are infinite?\n\n- $A = \\{n \\in \\mathbb{Z} \\: : \\: n^2-9 \\geq 0 \\}$\n\n- $B = \\{m \\in \\mathbb{Z} \\: : \\: m^2-9 \\leq 0 \\}$\n\n- $C = \\{q \\in \\mathbb{Q} \\: : \\: q^2-9 \\leq 0 \\}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072312/example-13171.json" "file_sha256" "774829e35df1d9f3c7846c69ff4bf95ef938ed1bec2c61f2e6c3bfbd578b1027"} {"question" "e-13171" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The cardinality of a finite set is the number of elements contained within it. A set is infinite if it has infinitely many elements.\n\nLet's start by analyzing the parabola $y=x^2-9$ for $x\\in\\mathbb R.$\n\nFirst, we find the roots of the parabola:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-9 & =0 \\\\ (x-3)(x+3) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the roots are $x=-3$ and $x=3,$ and we can graph the parabola as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072312/assets/3c2fc2f1eeb1d4e92f7933a408dafe7c3a216aa3d899f4025c71407140d47de1.png)\n\nTherefore:\n\n- The solution to the inequality $x^2-9 \\geq 0$ for $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$ is \n\n$$\nx \\leq -3 \\quad\\text{or}\\quad x \\geq 3\n$$\n\nand can be expressed as $x \\in I_1 = \\left(-\\infty, -3\\right] \\cup \\left[3,\\infty\\right).$\n\n- Similarly, the solution to the inequality $x^2-9 \\leq 0$ for $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$ is \n\n$$\n-3 \\leq x \\leq 3\n$$\n\nand can be expressed as $x \\in I_2 = [-3,3].$\n\nWith these in mind, let's examine our sets:\n\n- The set $A$ is infinite. If $n \\in \\mathbb{Z},$ the solutions to the inequality $n^2-9 \\geq 0$ are all the integers that lie in the interval $I_1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nA = \\{ \\ldots, -4, -3 \\} \\cup \\{ 3, 4, \\ldots \\}.\n$$\n\n- The set $B$ is finite. If $m \\in \\mathbb{Z},$ the solutions to the inequality $m^2-9 \\leq 0$ are all the integers that lie in the interval $I_2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nB = \\{0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,\\pm 3\\}.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $|B| = 7,$ which is finite.\n\n- The set $C$ is infinite. If $q \\in \\mathbb{Q},$ the solutions to $q^2-9 \\leq 0$ are all the rational numbers that lie in the interval $I_2.$ Since any real interval of nonzero length contains infinitely many rational numbers, it follows that $C$ is infinite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$A$ and $C$ only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072312/example-13171.json" "file_sha256" "774829e35df1d9f3c7846c69ff4bf95ef938ed1bec2c61f2e6c3bfbd578b1027"} {"question" "e-13174" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following sets are infinite?\n\n- $A = \\{n \\in \\mathbb{Z} \\: : \\: \\sin (n\\pi) = 1 \\}$\n\n- $B = \\{q \\in \\mathbb{Q} \\: : \\: \\sin (q\\pi) = 1 \\}$\n\n- $C = \\{x \\in \\mathbb{R} \\: : \\: \\sin (x\\pi) = 1 \\}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072312/example-13174.json" "file_sha256" "dcd33963c352d17c0400e88978af47f98b3637a14431600d483220b9d50a7db6"} {"question" "e-13174" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The cardinality of a finite set is the number of elements contained within it. A set is infinite if it has infinitely many elements.\n\nWe start by finding the solutions to the equation $\\sin (x\\pi) = 1$ for $x\\in\\mathbb R{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\pi  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}+2m\\pi  \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{2}+2m,\\,m\\in Z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our sets:\n\n- The set $A$ is finite. There are no values of $m\\in\\mathbb Z$ such that $\\dfrac{1}{2} + 2m \\in\\mathbb Z.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nA = \\{\\}.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $|A| = 0,$ which is finite.\n\n- The set $B$ is infinite. Listing the elements of $B$ for different values of $m,$ we have\n\n$$\nB = \\left\\{\\ldots,-\\dfrac{3}{2},\\dfrac12, \\dfrac{5}{2}, \\dfrac{9}{2},\\ldots\\right\\}.\n$$\n\n- The set $C$ is infinite. Since $B$ is infinite, and $B \\subseteq C,$ it follows that $C$ is infinite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$B$ and $C$ only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072312/example-13174.json" "file_sha256" "dcd33963c352d17c0400e88978af47f98b3637a14431600d483220b9d50a7db6"} {"question" "e-13175" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following sets are infinite?\n\n- $A = \\left\\{f^{(n)}(x) = \\dfrac{\\textrm d^n}{\\textrm d x^n}(e^{5x})  \\: : \\:  n\\in \\mathbb{N}_0 \\right\\}$\n\n- $B = \\left\\{g^{(n)}(x) = \\dfrac{\\textrm d^n}{\\textrm d x^n}(6e^{-x})  \\: : \\:  n\\in \\mathbb{N}_0 \\right\\}$\n\n- $C =  \\left\\{h^{(n)}(x) = \\dfrac{\\textrm d^n}{\\textrm d x^n}(2e^{x+3})  \\: : \\:  n\\in \\mathbb{N}_0 \\right\\}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072312/example-13175.json" "file_sha256" "fe3564038c959737d53f0c96b656cc641a6b3171e5af71ad5cf69514e2fa5c61"} {"question" "e-13175" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The cardinality of a finite set is the number of elements contained within it. A set is infinite if it has infinitely many elements.\n\n- The set $A$ is infinite. Listing the first few elements of our set, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{(0)}(x) & ={e}^{5x} \\\\ {f}^{(1)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{5x})=5{e}^{5x} \\\\ {f}^{(2)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(5{e}^{5x})=25{e}^{5x} \\\\ {f}^{(3)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(25{e}^{5x})=125{e}^{5x} \\\\ ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we can write our set as follows:\n\n$$\nA = \\{e^{5x}, 5e^{5x}, 25e^{5x}, 125e^{5x}, \\ldots\\}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $A$ is infinite.\n\n- The set $B$ is finite. Listing the first few elements of our set, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{(0)}(x) & =6{e}^{-x} \\\\ {g}^{(1)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(6{e}^{-x})=-6{e}^{-x} \\\\ {g}^{(2)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-6{e}^{-x})=6{e}^{-x} \\\\ {g}^{(3)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(6{e}^{-x})=-6{e}^{-x} \\\\ ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $B$ contains only two elements:\n\n$$\nB = \\{6e^{-x}, -6e^{-x}\\}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $|B| = 2,$ which is finite.\n\n- The set $C$ is also finite. Listing the first few elements of our set, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{(0)}(x) & =2{e}^{x+3} \\\\ {h}^{(1)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{e}^{x+3})=2{e}^{x+3} \\\\ {h}^{(2)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{e}^{x+3})=2{e}^{x+3} \\\\ {h}^{(3)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{e}^{x+3})=2{e}^{x+3} \\\\ ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $C$ contains only one element:\n\n$$\nC = \\{ 2e^{x+3} \\}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $|C| = 1,$ which is finite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$A$ only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072312/example-13175.json" "file_sha256" "fe3564038c959737d53f0c96b656cc641a6b3171e5af71ad5cf69514e2fa5c61"}] "usage" {"e-13174" [] "e-13175" [] "q-181668" [] "q-181740" [] "q-181650" [] "q-181597" [] "q-181536" [] "q-181599" [] "q-181674" [] "e-13171" [] "q-181643" [] "q-181768" []} "basis" 2635 "retractions" [[17592186306622 :question/worked-solution "The cardinality of a finite set is the number of elements contained within it. A set is infinite if it has infinitely many elements. \n\nLet's start by analyzing the parabola $y=-{x}^{2}+1$ for $x\\in R.$\n\nFirst, we find the roots of the parabola:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}^{2}+1 & =0 \\\\ -({x}^{2}-1) & =0 \\\\ -(x-1)(x+1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the roots are $x=-1$ and $x=1,$ and we can graph the parabola as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040284/assets/0699524ff20f871c9eeca93faa9934832b8e5816fd23b46862e8b01d48e7d86b.png)\n\nTherefore:\n\n- The solution to the inequality $-{x}^{2}+1>0$ for $x\\in R$ is \n\n$$\n-1<x<1\n$$\n\nand can be expressed as $x\\in {I}_{1}=(-1,1).$\n\n- Similarly, the solution to the inequality $-{x}^{2}+1<0$ for $x\\in R$ is \n\n$$\nx<-1\\,\\text{or}\\,x>1\n$$\n\nand can be expressed as $x\\in {I}_{2}=(-\\infty ,-1)\\cup (1,\\infty ).$\n\nWith these in mind, let's examine our sets:\n\n- The set $A$ is finite. If $n\\in Z,$ the solutions to the inequality $-{n}^{2}+1>0$ are all the integers that lie in the interval ${I}_{1}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nA={0}.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $|A|=1,$ which is finite.\n\n- The set $B$ is infinite. If $m\\in Z,$ the solutions to the inequality $-{m}^{2}+1<0$ are all the integers that lie in the interval ${I}_{2}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nB={…,-3,-2}\\cup {2,3,…}.\n$$\n\n- The set $C$ is infinite. If $q\\in Q,$ the solutions to $-{q}^{2}+1<0$ are all the rational numbers that lie in the interval ${I}_{2}.$ Since any real interval of nonzero length contains infinitely many rational numbers, it follows that $C$ is infinite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$B$ and $C$ only.\""] [17592186198711 :question/worked-solution "The cardinality of a finite set is the number of elements contained within it. A set is infinite if it has infinitely many elements. \n\nLet's start by analyzing the parabola $y={x}^{2}-5x+6$ for $x\\in R.$\n\nFirst, we find the roots of the parabola:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-5x+6 & =0 \\\\ (x-2)(x-3) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the roots are $x=2$ and $x=3,$ and we can graph the parabola as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040284/assets/0a42815f626652c332862a38a3812463a9407605329bd81df25f11274b26ce6f.png)\n\nTherefore:\n\n- The solution to the inequality ${x}^{2}-5x+6>0$ for $x\\in R$ is \n\n$$\nx<2\\,\\text{or}\\,x>3\n$$\n\nand can be expressed as $x\\in {I}_{1}=(-\\infty ,2)\\cup (3,\\infty ).$\n\n- Similarly, the solution to the inequality ${x}^{2}-5x+6<0$ for $x\\in R$ is \n\n$$\n2<x<3\n$$\n\nand can be expressed as $x\\in {I}_{2}=(2,3).$\n\nWith these in mind, let's examine our sets:\n\n- The set $A$ is infinite. If $n\\in Z,$ the solutions to the inequality ${n}^{2}-5n+6>0$ are all the integers that lie in the interval ${I}_{1}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nA={…,0,1}\\cup {4,5,…}.\n$$\n\n- The set $B$ is finite. If $m\\in Z,$ the solutions to the inequality ${m}^{2}-5m+6<0$ are all the integers that lie in the interval ${I}_{2}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nB={}.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $|B|=0,$ which is finite.\n\n- The set $C$ is infinite. If $q\\in Q,$ the solutions to ${q}^{2}-5q+6<0$ are all the rational numbers that lie in the interval ${I}_{2}.$ Since any real interval of nonzero length contains infinitely many rational numbers, it follows that $C$ is infinite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$A$ and $C$ only.\""] [17592186306629 :question/worked-solution "The cardinality of a finite set is the number of elements contained within it. A set is infinite if it has infinitely many elements. \n\nLet's start by analyzing the parabola $y={x}^{2}-2x$ for $x\\in R.$\n\nFirst, we find the roots of the parabola:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-2x & =0 \\\\ x(x-2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the roots are $x=0$ and $x=2,$ and we can graph the parabola as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040284/assets/496c6d53993ca6cae499f617bca42dbc42e4c57bffa278da6312a5b1723bdcbc.png)\n\nTherefore:\n\n- The solution to the inequality ${x}^{2}-2x\\le 0$ for $x\\in R$ is \n\n$$\n0\\le x\\le 2\n$$\n\nand can be expressed as $x\\in {I}_{1}=[0,2].$\n\n- Similarly, the solution to the inequality ${x}^{2}-2x\\ge 0$ for $x\\in R$ is \n\n$$\nx\\le 0\\,\\text{or}\\,x\\ge 2\n$$\n\nand can be expressed as $x\\in {I}_{2}=(-\\infty ,0]\\cup [2,\\infty ).$\n\nWith these in mind, let's examine our sets:\n\n- The set $A$ is finite. If $n\\in Z,$ the solutions to the inequality ${n}^{2}-2n\\le 0$ are all the integers that lie in the interval ${I}_{1}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nA={0,1,2}.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $|A|=3,$ which is finite.\n\n- The set $B$ is infinite. If $m\\in Z,$ the solutions to the inequality ${m}^{2}-2m\\ge 0$ are all the integers that lie in the interval ${I}_{2}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nB={…,-1,0}\\cup {2,3,…}.\n$$\n\n- The set $C$ is infinite. If $q\\in Q,$ the solutions to ${q}^{2}-2q\\le 0$ are all the rational numbers that lie in the interval ${I}_{1}.$ Since any real interval of nonzero length contains infinitely many rational numbers, it follows that $C$ is infinite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$B$ and $C$ only.\""] [17592186198719 :question/worked-solution "The cardinality of a finite set is the number of elements contained within it. A set is infinite if it has infinitely many elements. \n\nWe start by writing down the solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=0$ for $x\\in R:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+m\\pi ,\\,m\\in Z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our sets:\n\n- The set $A$ is finite. There are no values of $m\\in Z$ such that $\\frac{\\pi }{2}+m\\pi \\in Z.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nA={}.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $|A|=0,$ which is finite.\n\n- The set $B$ is finite. Similar to the above, there are no values of $m\\in Q$ such that $\\frac{\\pi }{2}+m\\pi \\in Q.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nB={}.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $|B|=0,$ which is finite.\n\n- The set $C$ is infinite. Listing the elements of $C$ for different values of $m,$ we have\n\n$$\nC={…,-\\frac{3\\pi }{2},-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{2},…}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$C$ only.\""] [17592186198084 :question/worked-solution "The cardinality of a finite set is the number of elements contained within it. A set is infinite if it has infinitely many elements.\n\nLet's start by analyzing the parabola $y=x^2-9$ for $x\\in\\mathbb R.$\n\nFirst, we find the roots of the parabola:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-9 & =0 \\\\ (x-3)(x+3) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the roots are $x=-3$ and $x=3,$ and we can graph the parabola as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040284/assets/3c2fc2f1eeb1d4e92f7933a408dafe7c3a216aa3d899f4025c71407140d47de1.png)\n\nTherefore:\n\n- The solution to the inequality $x^2-9 \\geq 0$ for $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$ is \n\n$$\nx \\leq -3 \\quad\\text{or}\\quad x \\geq 3\n$$\n\nand can be expressed as $x \\in I_1 = \\left(-\\infty, -3\\right] \\cup \\left[3,\\infty\\right).$\n\n- Similarly, the solution to the inequality $x^2-9 \\leq 0$ for $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$ is \n\n$$\n-3 \\leq x \\leq 3\n$$\n\nand can be expressed as $x \\in I_2 = [-3,3].$\n\nWith these in mind, let's examine our sets:\n\n- The set $A$ is infinite. If $n \\in \\mathbb{Z},$ the solutions to the inequality $n^2-9 \\geq 0$ are all the integers that lie in the interval $I_1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nA = \\{ \\ldots, -4, -3 \\} \\cup \\{ 3, 4, \\ldots \\}.\n$$\n\n- The set $B$ is finite. If $m \\in \\mathbb{Z},$ the solutions to the inequality $m^2-9 \\leq 0$ are all the integers that lie in the interval $I_2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nB = \\{0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,\\pm 3\\}.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $|B| = 7,$ which is finite.\n\n- The set $C$ is infinite. If $q \\in \\mathbb{Q},$ the solutions to $q^2-9 \\leq 0$ are all the rational numbers that lie in the interval $I_2.$ Since any real interval of nonzero length contains infinitely many rational numbers, it follows that $C$ is infinite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$A$ and $C$ only.\""]] "immutable_answers" [17592186195266 17592186195267 17592186195268 17592186195269 17592186195270 17592186195278 17592186195279 17592186195280 17592186195281 17592186195282 17592186195284 17592186195285 17592186195286 17592186195287 17592186195288 17592186195319 17592186195320 17592186195321 17592186195322 17592186195323 17592186294273 17592186294274 17592186294275 17592186294276 17592186294277 17592186306569 17592186306570 17592186306571 17592186306572 17592186306573 1759218